//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="false\ntrue\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conf.resolve.ambient, type.fn.value
//!
//! wolf-lang#400 (s171), the cross-module half — the shape sc50 hit
//! through `std.list.sort_by`, and the reason a caller cannot defend
//! against the bug: the LIBRARY picks the parameter's name, and the
//! entry module happens to declare a top-level fn with that name.
//!
//! `cmp.apply` calls its own `le` parameter. The checked machine's
//! name-only net searched every module for a body called `le` and
//! took the smallest body index, which is this file's `fn le` — so
//! the library called the CALLER's unrelated function. Renaming
//! either side made the divergence vanish, which is what kept it
//! hidden. Correct is `ge(1, 2)` then `ge(2, 1)` — `false`, `true`;
//! the buggy machine printed this file's `le` instead, `true` then
//! `false`, so BOTH lines flip and neither is a coincidence.

use cmp

fn le(a: int, b: int) -> bool { a <= b }

fn ge(a: int, b: int) -> bool { a >= b }

fn main() -> !int {
    print("{cmp.apply(ge, 1, 2)}")
    print("{cmp.apply_swapped(ge, 1, 2)}")
    0
}
